---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy notice: MemJudge"
  description="What personal data MemJudge handles, why, for how long, and your rights."
>
  <article class="legal">
    <h1>Privacy notice</h1>
    <p class="muted">Last updated 28 October 2026</p>
    <p>
      MemJudge collects as little personal data as it can. You can read the whole site without
      signing in, and it uses no analytics, adverts or tracking cookies.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      Bit Robotics Ltd (company number 11680576, registered office 13 Freeland Park, Wareham Road,
      Lytchett House, Poole, Dorset, BH16 6FA) is responsible for your data. Contact:{" "}
      <a href="mailto:admin@memjudge.org">admin@memjudge.org</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>2. What we handle, and why</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Visiting the site.</strong> Cloudflare, which hosts the site, processes your IP
        address and request details to deliver the pages and protect them from attacks. Your browser
        keeps your theme choice and, on the Run page, your sign-in for that tab and the id of your
        last run. These stay on your device and only make the features you use work, so we do not
        ask for cookie consent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Signing in.</strong> GitHub shares your public profile when you sign in. We use only
        your numeric account id, and we store only a keyed hash of it: never the id itself, your
        username, name or email.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Starting a run.</strong> We keep a run record: that hash, the run's settings and
        progress, your affiliation answer, the date of the terms you agreed to, and timestamps. The
        runner's request logs record your IP address. Your API keys are encrypted for the run's
        jobs, deleted when a job reads them (unused ones within 3 days) and never logged. The jobs
        log their progress with your keys removed; only you see those lines, on the run's form.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Published results.</strong> A result holds no personal data except your affiliation
        answer (yes or no, and which system), and it is not linked to your GitHub account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Emails.</strong> If you email us, we keep the correspondence to deal with it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Our lawful basis is legitimate interests: running a free public benchmark, applying its
      fair-use limits, keeping it secure and handling reports. We do not sell data or use it for
      marketing.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Who else handles it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>GitHub, for sign-in.</li>
      <li>Google Cloud, which runs the runner in the United States (Iowa).</li>
      <li>Cloudflare, which serves the website and the published result files.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These providers may process data in the United States. Transfers rely on the UK–US data bridge
      where the provider is certified under it, and otherwise on the UK's standard contract terms.
      Your memory service and judge provider (Anthropic or OpenRouter) receive the benchmark data
      under your own accounts and their own privacy terms; that data is dataset text, not personal
      data about you.
    </p>

    <h2>4. How long we keep it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Run records: deleted automatically 90 days after the run starts.</li>
      <li>Daily-limit counters, which hold the same hash: deleted after 30 days.</li>
      <li>The runner's request logs and the run jobs' logs: 30 days.</li>
      <li>Cloudflare's logs: as Cloudflare's own policies set.</li>
      <li>
        Published results: for as long as the leaderboard runs, unless removed under the{" "}
        <a href="/corrections/">Corrections policy</a>.
      </li>
      <li>Emails: as long as we need them to deal with what you asked.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask to see a copy of your data, to correct or delete it, and to restrict or object to
      how we use it. Email <a href="mailto:admin@memjudge.org">admin@memjudge.org</a>. We store only
      a hash of your GitHub id, so tell us your GitHub username and we will find your records from
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Complaints</h2>
    <p>
      If you are unhappy with how we handle your data, email{" "}
      <a href="mailto:admin@memjudge.org">admin@memjudge.org</a> first. We acknowledge a complaint
      within 30 days and tell you what we did about it. You can also complain to the Information
      Commissioner's Office at <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/">ico.org.uk</a> or on
      0303 123 1113.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Children and changes</h2>
    <p>
      The hosted runner is for people aged 18 or over. We may update this notice; the date at the
      top shows the current version.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
